At the 10 GB / 0.3 kWh point on ogbn-products, TADP-HABLS attains 83.8 \(\pm\) 0.2 \% test accuracy versus 82.9 \(\pm\) 0.3 \% for DPA-HABLS, while consuming 1.42 \(\pm\) 0.05 kJ versus 2.29 \(\pm\) 0.07 kJ. Across all 27 (budget, dataset) combinations every baseline point is strictly dominated: on average +0.7 pp accuracy and \(-\)38 \% JEPE relative to DPA-HABLS, \(-\)55 \% versus NS-25. Under the tight 6 GB cap accuracy retains 95 \% (products) and 93 \% (papers100M) of the 14 GB score; baselines lose 7-12 \%. Ablations show \(-\)M1 doubles inter-batch Jaccard (0.18\(\rightarrow\)0.40) and costs 1.1 pp accuracy; \(-\)M4 raises energy by 18 \%.  
                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               ^
Warning 13 in paper.tex line 188: Intersentence spacing (`\@') should perhaps be used.
On Reddit-Temporal the inter-GPU neighbour overlap stays \(\le\) 0.19 for the full 48 h with TADP-HABLS; HABLS drifts from 0.22 to 0.47 after 12 h; GraphSAINT stabilises at 0.51. Coverage of new high-attention vertices per window averages 64.3 \%, matching the \(1 - 1/e\) theoretical bound. The look-ahead GRU removes 21.7 \% of the forward-pass latency, keeping throughput within 1.8 \% of GraphSAINT, whereas HABLS slows by 24 \% after 30 k steps. Area under the accuracy-time curve is +1.3 \% in the first 15 k batches and final accuracy is +0.8 pp over HABLS, +1.6 pp over GraphSAINT. Energy per processed edge falls by 35 \% versus GraphSAINT and 18 \% versus HABLS.  
                                                                                                       ^
